  year      = {2024},
  doi       = {10.1109/CBMS61543.2024.00040},
  internal_note = {Third-party, peer-reviewed use of Pulse as a source of machine-learning training data --- the citation the repository lacked (preprint_npj/README.md L104-112 recorded that no verified citation existed for "clinical-AI training" applied to Pulse as of 2026-05-23). Verbatim from the abstract, read at arXiv:2411.07971 on 2026-08-03: "The Pulse Physiology Engine's respiratory dynamics simulator is used to establish a repeatable benchmark, gather simulated data, and quantitatively compare these controllers." Controllers compared are ARDSnet-protocol, optimal-control, and learned latent dynamics represented as neural networks, framed as a Markov decision process for ARDS ventilation. Authors are Stanford (Kochenderfer group), NOT Kitware, so this is genuinely third-party unlike the Kitware-authored ML abstracts on pulse.kitware.com/published.html (triage LLM alignment, deep time-series predictor, ML cardiovascular-collapse prediction). Metadata verified at Crossref 2026-08-03: title, 5 authors, container "2024 IEEE 37th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS)", pages 192-198, IEEE, proceedings-article, published 2024-06-26. Five authors exactly, so npj's ">five -> et al." rule does not apply and all five are listed. Kept as @misc because sn-nature.bst stack-pops on @inproceedings (same handling as lundberg2017 and webb2022vent). VERIFICATION DEPTH: FULL TEXT READ 2026-08-10 (7 pages, arXiv:2411.07971v1); this note previously read "abstract read verbatim; full text NOT read" and that is no longer true. What the full read established, and what the Discussion now states: 100 simulated patients run twice under a random policy give 200 runs of 96 actions, i.e. (96-1) x 200 = 19,000 state-action-state triplets (their arithmetic, verbatim); those train an autoencoder plus a latent dynamics model; that model is then used "in lieu of Pulse" to simulate futures; and it is judged by how well it approximates "Pulse's respiratory dynamics", with "validating that our approaches trained on simulated data generalize to real-world patient-ventilator data" listed as open future work. CAUTION, do not write "uniform sampling": the random policy draws each setting from a uniform range (FiO2 (0,1), Pinsp (1,30), Tinsp (0.1,3), RR (1,30), PEEP (1,25), slope (0,1)) but the paper also states that "physically impossible actions are made valid (e.g. PEEP cannot be higher than Pinsp, so it is adjusted down)", so the effective distribution is not uniform on those bounds. SCOPE GAP, now disclosed in limit (iii): their six-setting action vector is pressure-targeted, so only FiO2, RR and PEEP are inputs this atlas sweeps -- and their RANGES exceed ours on all three (FiO2 0.21-1.0, RR 8-20, PEEP 0-20 here). Their timestep is 30 simulated minutes, far beyond the 120 s / 300 s window of the hyperoxia finding, so that finding does NOT transfer to their data; an earlier draft of this analysis wrongly claimed it did.}
