  journal   = {The American Journal of Clinical Nutrition},
  volume    = {40},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {168--182},
  year      = {1984},
  pmid      = {6741850},
  internal_note = {Cited as the source of the basal-metabolic-rate equations Pulse v4.3.1 implements: SetupPatient.cpp:768-775 carries the comment "The basal metabolic rate is determined from the Harris-Benedict formula" and the Doxygen tag \cite roza1984metabolic. VERIFICATION DEPTH: primary PDF obtained and read 2026-08-15; every bibliographic field above taken from the article's own self-citation on its first page, verbatim "Am J Clin Nutr 1984;40: 168-182". No DOI is printed in the 1984 article, so none is recorded here; PMID verified via PubMed. THE EQUATIONS, from p. 171, which prints them twice: Equation 3, men (n = 168), REE = 88.362 + 4.799 (ht) + 13.397 (wt) - 5.677 (age); Equation 6, women (n = 169), REE = 447.593 + 3.098 (ht) + 9.247 (wt) - 4.330 (age). These are the REVISED equations, not the 1919 originals, which appear on the same page as Equations 1 and 4 with different constants (men 66.473, women 655.096) -- so the text must say "the Roza-Shizgal revision", never "the Harris-Benedict equations" unqualified. Read from the page image via pdftoppm, not from OCR alone: the OCR of this PDF demonstrably confuses B and 8, rendering "BCM" as "8CM". IMPLEMENTATION DIVERGENCE, documented in Supplement S10 and audit/pulse_sex_conditioned_parameters.md: Pulse uses 88.632 for the male intercept where this paper reports 88.362, a digit transposition present at two loci in the engine (SetupPatient.cpp:771, live; EnergyModel.cpp:752, dead code). Effect on the male reference body is +0.27 kcal/day, 0.015 %, immaterial to every comparison in this work. WHAT MUST NOT BE WRITTEN: the authors' own conclusion is that resting energy expenditure "is directly related to the size of the BCM and is independent of age and sex", the sex term being a body-composition proxy -- so this reference does not support describing Pulse as modelling a sex-specific metabolism.}
}
